  "slug": "x10-command-console",
  "title": "BSR/X10 Command Console (Original)",
  "year": "1978",
  "subtitle": "The first home-automation controller: a wedge of plastic with a rotary house-code dial and a grid of unit buttons that commands your whole house over the power line",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's home-automation and environment-control thread (Butler in a Box voice control, the deferred Unity Systems Home Manager) is missing its founding physical controller. The original BSR/X10 Command Console (1978) is the first general-purpose home automation network controller, developed by Pico Electronics in Glenrothes, Scotland. Its interaction is a strange, embodied ritual: no screen, no software — you select a house code by turning a rotary dial (A–P), press unit buttons (1–16) to address a lamp or appliance, then On/Off/Dim/Bright. The entire 'programming' of a smart home lives in the physical position of a plastic dial and the button you press, and the data travels as 120 kHz bursts across the mains wiring at ~20 bits per second, one bit per zero crossing. It is the ancestor of every smart-home control surface and a physically-grounded, commercially-massive artifact of the pre-screen era.",
  "search_terms": [
    "BSR X10 command console 1978 rotary dial home automation",
    "Pico Electronics X10 first home automation controller",
    "X10 powerline control lamp module 1978 RadioShack Sears",
    "X10 house code unit code dial interface"
  ],
  "research": {
    "title": "BSR/X10 Command Console",
    "year": "1978",
    "by": "Pico Electronics (Glenrothes, Scotland) / X10 Ltd / BSR",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Home Automation", "Input", "Powerline Control", "Physical"],
    "overview": "X10 was developed starting in 1975 by Pico Electronics of Glenrothes, Scotland, as the first general-purpose home automation network technology. The system appeared in RadioShack and Sears stores in 1978, sold by X10 Ltd. in partnership with BSR (Birmingham Sound Reproducers). The initial kit was a 16-channel command console plus a lamp module and an appliance module; a wall switch module and a timer followed. The concept grew out of a 1974 collaboration on the ADC Accutrac 4000 turntable, which Pico engineered with BSR and which could be operated by an ultrasonic remote — the seed idea of controlling lights and appliances remotely.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "A physical dial is the entire programming interface",
        "text": "The original command console has no display and no software metaphor. The user selects one of sixteen house codes (A through P) with a rotary dial, then presses a unit button (1 through 16) to address a specific module, then presses On, Off, Dim, or Bright. The complete configuration of a home lighting system lives in the physical position of a dial and the sequence of buttons pressed. To command a lamp, the cashier of the household spins the dial to its letter and stabs a number — a strange, embodied ritual that prefigures (and is the inverse of) every modern smart-home app."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Data travels over the mains at 20 bits per second",
        "text": "Signals are encoded as brief 120 kHz bursts on the household power-line carrier, sent during the quiet zero crossings of the 50/60 Hz AC waveform. Each bit takes two zero crossings, so the effective data rate is around 20 bits per second — slow enough that X10 is confined to turning devices on and off or very simple dimming. An address (house code + unit code) followed by a command is transmitted twice for reliability; several units can be addressed before a single command applies to all of them. This is computing folded onto the electrical wiring itself."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A small ecosystem of physical modules",
        "text": "The console commands a set of plug-in physical modules that are themselves tiny interfaces: a lamp module (a solid-state TRIAC dimmer you can also switch locally), an appliance module (an impulse relay for motors and fluorescent loads), a wall-switch module, and later a timer. Each module is configured to respond to one of the 256 possible house/unit addresses, chosen by its own physical dial or switch. The whole system is a network of objects that wear their configuration on their bodies."
      }
    ],
